arm64: canonicalize unsupported TCO in exception frames

The Harmony ARM identity advertises ID_AA64PFR1_EL1.MTE=0 and the guest
kernel is built without CONFIG_ARM64_MTE.  PSTATE.TCO is therefore outside
the guest contract.  A physical MTE-capable KVM CPU nevertheless sets TCO on
exception entry, while the HVF substrate leaves it clear.  A later nested
exception copied that substrate bit into struct pt_regs: the otherwise
identical M5 RAM images differed by exactly PSR_TCO_BIT in one saved pstate.

Under the cooperative Harmony clock configuration, clear TCO from SPSR_EL1
before Linux publishes it in an exception frame.  This is an architectural
no-op for the admitted no-MTE guest and prevents an unsupported host feature
from becoming persistent guest RAM or snapshot state.

This patch is Linux-kernel code (GPL-2.0), carried as a diff under the Harmony
repository's designated guest-kernel patch exception.

---
--- a/arch/arm64/kernel/entry.S
+++ b/arch/arm64/kernel/entry.S
@@ -281,6 +281,13 @@ alternative_else_nop_endif
 	.endif /* \el == 0 */
 	mrs	x22, elr_el1
 	mrs	x23, spsr_el1
+#ifdef CONFIG_HARMONY_ARM_PVCLOCK
+	/*
+	 * FEAT_MTE is absent from Harmony's guest identity.  Do not retain a
+	 * physical CPU's exception-entry TCO residue in struct pt_regs.
+	 */
+	bic	x23, x23, #PSR_TCO_BIT
+#endif
 	stp	lr, x21, [sp, #S_LR]
 
 	/*
